## The mental model, restated

Digital sizes behave like built-in numeric types, not like full-fledged types with strict type-matching arithmetic. `int`, `long`, and `double` all participate in arithmetic with each other following well-understood rules about representation and truncation. Adding `0.5` to an `int` silently truncates because the int doesn't have sub-1 resolution to spare, and that's accepted behavior — the language doesn't require a cast because the domain is small enough and the rules are uniform enough that the behavior is predictable.

Digital sizes work the same way. A `GiB` has resolution no finer than 1 GiB. Adding `2_MiB` to a `GiB` does nothing — not because the compiler rejected it, but because the MiB value is below the GiB's resolution floor and rounds away. That's the correct behavior for a unit system that represents magnitudes at their native resolution. If the user needed sub-GiB precision, they wouldn't be holding a `GiB` in the first place.

And the "hint is in the name" point is the one I'd been missing. `MiB` and `MB` existing side-by-side is itself a signpost — if you're mixing them, you're either doing it deliberately (in which case the truncation is expected), or you don't understand the distinction (in which case you need to learn, and forcing an explicit cast won't teach you any faster than letting you see the truncated result).

## What this means for the compound operators

Drop the `requires` clause entirely. `operator+=` and `operator-=` accept any `DigitalSize` specialization, cast it to the left-hand side's unit (which may truncate), and add or subtract. Same behavior the rest of the integer type system exhibits — no special cases, no compile-time rejection of "lossy" combinations.

Here's the revised form:

```cpp
template <uint64_t IncomingUnitBytes>
constexpr DigitalSize& operator+=(const DigitalSize<IncomingUnitBytes>& other) noexcept
{
    m_count += DigitalSizeCast<DigitalSize>::cast(other).count();
    return *this;
}

template <uint64_t IncomingUnitBytes>
constexpr DigitalSize& operator-=(const DigitalSize<IncomingUnitBytes>& other)
{
    const ReprType rhs = DigitalSizeCast<DigitalSize>::cast(other).count();

    if (rhs > m_count) {
        throw std::underflow_error{
            "DigitalSize::operator-=: subtraction would produce a negative result"
        };
    }

    m_count -= rhs;
    return *this;
}
```

Short and direct. The `DigitalSizeCast<DigitalSize>::cast(other)` call does whatever conversion is needed — lossless when the right-hand side is coarser, truncating when the right-hand side is finer, also truncating when the two are in different size systems. That truncation is the natural behavior of the unit arithmetic, not a problem to guard against.

**A few points worth flagging:**

**`operator+=` is `noexcept` again.** Without the `requires` clause, I could have argued otherwise — but honestly, adding any count to `m_count` can't throw. The underlying arithmetic is `uint64_t` addition, which wraps on overflow silently (and we're not choosing to guard against overflow on the hot path). So `noexcept` is honest.

**`operator-=` still throws on underflow.** This one I'd keep, even in the new permissive framing. Underflow isn't the same as truncation — truncation is "the value you added doesn't move the needle at this resolution," which is a documented consequence of holding a coarse unit. Underflow is "the result would be negative in a world where negative doesn't exist," which is a different failure mode. One is expected behavior of a coarse unit type; the other is a genuine error in the caller's logic. The asymmetry is legitimate.

**No `static_assert` or `requires` anywhere in these two functions now.** They just work, for any valid `DigitalSize`.

That's all the change. The binary `operator+`/`operator-` we already fixed stay as they are — the cross-system `Bytes` branch is still the right call there because binary arithmetic returns a fresh result and we have the freedom to pick a precise representation. Compound assignment doesn't have that freedom, and the symmetric policy change reflects that: binary can be precise, compound can't — because compound mutates an existing committed type.

