## The approach

`std::declval` and `decltype` are exactly right for this. `decltype(std::declval<UnitsT>() + std::declval<UnitsT>())` gives us the return type of the `+` operation without needing an actual instance. We can then check that return type against a predicate.

We can't check "is this a `DigitalSize`" — but we *can* check "does this return type look like a digital size unit" using the same shape predicates the outer concept already uses. Namely: does it have a `ByteRatio` that's a `std::ratio`, does it have an unsigned integral `ReprType`, etc.

**There's a recursion concern to address up front.** If we check "the return type of `+` satisfies `digital_size_units`," we're writing a recursive concept. `digital_size_units<UnitsT>` would require `digital_size_unit_arithmetic<UnitsT>`, which would require `digital_size_units<ReturnType>`, which would require `digital_size_unit_arithmetic<ReturnType>`, and so on. Concepts can't recurse on themselves — the compiler will flag this as a cyclic dependency.

The way out is to check a **weaker predicate** on the return type. Not "is a full digital_size_units type" but "is shape-compatible with the unit system" — which is precisely what `has_byte_ratio_spec_v` plus a `ReprType` check already express. That's a non-recursive check that captures what we actually care about: the return type is a member of the unit family, with the structural requirements that define membership.

## The concept

```cpp
template <typename UnitsT>
concept digital_size_unit_arithmetic =
    requires(UnitsT u, const typename UnitsT::ReprType i) {
        // Relational operators — return bool
        { u < u }  -> std::same_as<bool>;
        { u > u }  -> std::same_as<bool>;
        { u == u } -> std::same_as<bool>;
        { u <= u } -> std::same_as<bool>;
        { u >= u } -> std::same_as<bool>;

        // Scalar arithmetic — preserves the unit type
        { u * i } -> std::same_as<UnitsT>;
        { u / i } -> std::same_as<UnitsT>;

        // Same-unit division — yields a dimensionless count
        { u / u } -> std::same_as<typename UnitsT::ReprType>;

        // Unit arithmetic — returns some DigitalSize-shaped type
        // (not necessarily UnitsT; could be a finer unit or Bytes)
        requires traits::has_byte_ratio_spec_v<
            decltype(std::declval<UnitsT>() + std::declval<UnitsT>())
        >;
        requires traits::has_byte_ratio_spec_v<
            decltype(std::declval<UnitsT>() - std::declval<UnitsT>())
        >;

        // Compound assignment — returns reference to this type
        { u += u } -> std::same_as<UnitsT&>;
        { u -= u } -> std::same_as<UnitsT&>;
        { u *= i } -> std::same_as<UnitsT&>;
        { u /= i } -> std::same_as<UnitsT&>;
    };
```

